    "result_text": "The build succeeds and the test passes. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` endpoint now honours an optional `Idempotency-Key` header: when a create repeats with a key already seen, the originally created owner is returned with **200 OK** instead of creating a duplicate (which the duplicate/identity rules would otherwise reject with 409).\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the `Idempotency-Key` header as a first-class, optional parameter on the `addOwner` operation. This flows through the OpenAPI generator into the `OwnersApi` interface as `@RequestHeader(value = \"Idempotency-Key\", required = false) String idempotencyKey`, so the header is part of the API contract rather than being read out-of-band.\n\n2. **`IdempotentOwnerStore.java`** (new `@Component`) \u2014 A small unit with a single responsibility: remembering which owner id was created under a given idempotency key (`find(key)` / `remember(key, owner)`). It stores only the id and keeps no other logic, matching the codebase's pattern of focused per-concern helper components. It's backed by a `ConcurrentHashMap` for thread safety.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 \n   - Before doing any creation work, if a key is present and already known, it re-reads the original owner via the existing `clinicService.findOwnerById` and returns it with `200 OK`.\n   - After a genuine save, it records the key \u2192 owner id mapping, then proceeds with the existing audit-log/`201 CREATED` path unchanged.\n\nThe replay path short-circuits before the duplicate checks, save, and audit logging, so a repeat neither creates a second record nor emits a spurious \"Owner created\" audit entry. The original creation flow and all other endpoints are untouched.",
